#################################################################
#    Annotations
#################################################################

:AmorphousGrainBoundaryPhase <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the amorphous grain boundary phase used for? — provides magnetic isolation between SmFe12 grains"@en .


:AndersonImpurityModel rdfs:comment "The Anderson impurity model describes hybridisation of localised 4f states with conduction electrons in rare-earth PM compounds; it is IN_SCOPE as an electronic-structure model and a GAP because emmo:ElectronicModel has no sub-class for quantum impurity models distinct from DFT or tight-binding approaches."@en ;
                       rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                       rdfs:label "anderson impurity model" ;
                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "anderson impurity model"@en .


:AndersonModel <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the Anderson model used for? — from which the Kondo model is derived via the Schrieffer-Wolff transformation"@en .


:ClassicalHeisenbergModel rdfs:comment "The classical Heisenberg model is an atomistic spin Hamiltonian parameterized by exchange coupling constants Jij used to compute Curie temperature; it is a canonical emmo:AtomisticModel for spin-lattice simulations, and no existing pm: class represents this Hamiltonian model."@en ;
                          rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                          rdfs:label "classical heisenberg model" ;
                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "classical heisenberg model"@en .


:CoherentPotentialApproximation <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How are electronic and magnetic properties of disordered alloys computed? — effective medium theory used to compute electronic and magnetic properties of disordered alloys by replacing the random potential with an effective coherent potential"@en .


:CrystalFieldTheory rdfs:comment "Crystal field theory is a quantum-mechanical framework computing electrostatic interactions between rare-earth 4f electrons and the surrounding crystal lattice, directly relevant to magnetocrystalline anisotropy in permanent magnets; it is an emmo:ElectronicModel, and no existing pm: class captures this ligand-field approach."@en ;
                    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                    rdfs:label "crystal field theory" ;
                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is crystal field theory computed? — using point charge model to calculate the electrostatic interaction between rare-earth ion 4f electrons and surrounding crystal environment"@en ;
                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "crystal field theory"@en .


:DepinningCoercivity <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is depinning coercivity used for? — determining coercivity in the depinning-type magnetization reversal mechanism"@en .


:DipoleField <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the dipole field computed? — computed via the demagnetization tensor"@en .


:DomainNucleation rdfs:comment "Domain nucleation is a magnetisation-reversal mechanism central to coercivity modelling in permanent magnets; no existing pm: or magmo: class captures nucleation as a distinct process, requiring a new subclass under pm:MicromagneticsStep."@en ;
                  rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                  rdfs:label "domain nucleation" ;
                  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "domain nucleation"@en .


:DomainWallMotion rdfs:comment "Domain wall motion is a distinct magnetization-change process simulated in micromagnetics workflows (pm:MicromagneticsStep) but not represented as its own class; a new subclass is needed per the gap criterion."@en ;
                  rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                  rdfs:label "domain wall motion" ;
                  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is domain wall motion used for? — the primary mechanism of magnetization change in materials with well-defined domain structures"@en ;
                  <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "domain wall motion"@en .


:DomainWallPropagation rdfs:comment "Domain wall propagation is a magnetisation-reversal mechanism (Barkhausen jumps) central to coercivity in permanent magnets and modelled in micromagnetics; no existing pm: or magmo: class captures wall motion as a distinct process."@en ;
                       rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                       rdfs:label "domain wall propagation" ;
                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "domain wall propagation"@en .


:DynamicalMeanFieldTheory rdfs:comment "Dynamical mean-field theory (DMFT) is a many-body electronic-structure method treating local electron correlations beyond DFT, applied to Sm 4f electrons in permanent magnet calculations; it is a subclass of emmo:ElectronicModel, and no existing pm: class covers correlated-electron many-body methods."@en ;
                          rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                          rdfs:label "dynamical mean-field theory" ;
                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is dynamical mean-field theory computed? — applied here with the Hubbard I approximation for Sm 4f electrons and STPF solver for Co 3d electrons"@en ,
                                                                     "What is dynamical mean field theory used for? — combined with first-principles band structure calculations for finite-temperature magnetism"@en ;
                          <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "dynamical mean-field theory"@en .


:EffectiveAnisotropyField <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the effective anisotropy field used for? — used in the LLG equation to compute resonance frequency"@en .


:FccStructure <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What transformation must fcc structure undergo to acquire hard magnetic properties? — must be transformed to fct for hard magnetic properties"@en .


:FermiDiracSmearing <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How are Kohn-Sham states occupied in DFT calculations? — Fermi-Dirac distribution with width 0.01 eV used to occupy Kohn-Sham states in the DFT calculation"@en .


:ForceTheorem <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the force theorem used for? — used to calculate magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy by comparing band energies along different magnetization directions"@en .


:FullPotentialLinearMuffinTinOrbitalMethod <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the full-potential linear muffin-tin orbital method implemented? — implemented in RSPt"@en .


:FullPotentialLmtoMethod <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy computed using the full-potential LMTO method? — A full-potential linear muffin-tin orbital computational method (RSPt code) used to compute magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy with spin-orbit coupling treated post-SCF"@en .


:GaussianDistributionOfEasyAxes <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What does the Gaussian distribution of easy axes describe? — used to describe the angular spread of grain easy axes in oriented nanocrystalline magnets"@en .


:HeisenbergModelHamiltonian rdfs:comment "The Heisenberg model Hamiltonian is the classical spin Hamiltonian parameterising pairwise exchange interactions between atomic sites in PM spin-dynamics simulations; it is IN_SCOPE and a GAP because pm:SpinDynamicsConfiguration covers solver/configuration settings but not the Hamiltonian functional form as a model class."@en ;
                            rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                            rdfs:label "heisenberg model hamiltonian" ;
                            <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "heisenberg model hamiltonian"@en .


:HubbardIApproximation rdfs:comment "The Hubbard-I approximation is a many-body electronic-structure method for treating localised 4f electrons in rare-earth PM compounds; it is IN_SCOPE and a GAP because pm:ExchangeCorrelationFunctional covers DFT XC functionals but not beyond-DFT many-body impurity solvers."@en ;
                       rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                       rdfs:label "hubbard i approximation" ;
                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the Hubbard I approximation computed? — neglecting hybridization in the DMFT impurity problem, approximating the self-energy by the atomic self-energy"@en ;
                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "hubbard i approximation"@en .


:HubbardUPotential <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the Hubbard U potential computed? — A phenomenological on-site Coulomb correction term added to DFT to better describe strongly correlated d or f electrons"@en .


:InPlaneMagneticAnisotropy <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is in-plane magnetic anisotropy used for? — which is unfavorable for permanent magnet applications"@en .


:IntersiteExchangeCouplingParameter <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the intersite exchange coupling parameter computed? — calculated via linear-response to small-angle spin fluctuations"@en .


:IsothermalRemanentMagnetization <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is isothermal remanent magnetization used for? — used to construct Henkel plots"@en .


:MagneticExchangeConstant <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the magnetic exchange constant computed? — obtained via the Liechtenstein real-space approach using SPR-KKR"@en .


:MagneticForceTheorem rdfs:comment "The magnetic force theorem is a first-principles electronic-structure method for computing intersite exchange parameters Jij via second derivatives of total energy w.r.t. spin rotations; it fits under emmo:ElectronicModel as a quantum-mechanical computational approach, and no existing pm: or magmo: class captures this specific perturbative energy-derivative technique."@en ;
                      rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                      rdfs:label "magnetic force theorem" ;
                      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How are exchange parameters Jij computed using the magnetic force theorem? — computing intersite exchange parameters Jij from the second derivative of the total energy with respect to spin rotations, without performing full self-consistent calculations"@en ;
                      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "magnetic force theorem"@en .


:MagneticHyperfineField <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the magnetic hyperfine field measured? — measured by Mössbauer spectroscopy"@en ,
                                                                   "What is the magnetic hyperfine field used for? — used to estimate local magnetic moments"@en .


:MagneticPermeabilityOfVacuum <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the magnetic permeability of vacuum used for? — used in the magnetostatic potential equations for the conical magnet"@en .


:MagnetizationReversal rdfs:comment "Magnetization reversal is a core micromagnetic process simulated in pm:MicromagneticsStep workflows but is not itself an existing class; a new subclass under pm:MicromagneticsStep captures the switching process per the gap criterion."@en ;
                       rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                       rdfs:label "magnetization reversal" ;
                       <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "magnetization reversal"@en .


:MagnetostaticField <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the magnetostatic field used for? — contributing to spin disorder"@en .


:MonteCarloAlgorithm <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the Monte Carlo algorithm used for? — used to calculate equilibrium magnetic properties such as temperature-dependent magnetization by averaging over thermally accessible spin configurations"@en .


:MonteCarloStep <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is a Monte Carlo step defined? — corresponding to one attempted spin flip per lattice site on average"@en .


:MuffinTinApproximation <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the crystal potential approximated in the muffin-tin approximation? — crystal potential is spherically symmetric within non-overlapping spheres (muffin tins) centered on each atom and constant in the interstitial region"@en .


:NoncollinearMagneticCalculation <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is a noncollinear magnetic calculation performed? — A DFT calculation in which spin directions are not constrained to be parallel or antiparallel, allowing treatment of spin canting, spin spirals, and complex magnetic textures"@en .


:QuasiharmonicApproximation <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the quasiharmonic approximation computed? — sampling phonon modes at the Gamma point of a supercell, accounting for volume-dependent frequency changes"@en .


:SelfConsistentFieldCalculation <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "How is the self-consistent field calculation performed? — Iterative DFT procedure to solve the Kohn-Sham equations self-consistently to obtain the ground-state electron density and magnetic moments"@en .


:StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbert <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#note> "What is the stochastic Landau-Lifshitz-Gilbert method used for? — used in related coercivity studies"@en .


:StochasticLandauLifshitzGilbertEquation rdfs:comment "The stochastic LLG equation is an atomistic spin-dynamics equation of motion augmented with a Langevin noise term for thermal fluctuations; it extends the deterministic LLG and belongs under emmo:AtomisticModel, while pm:SpinDynamicsConfiguration could hold its configuration parameters but does not represent the model itself."@en ;
                                         rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/pm-multiscale> ;
                                         rdfs:label "stochastic landau-lifshitz-gilbert equation" ;
